{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/a2-everyday-australia-part-2/audio/main-067e0ef6b160.mp3","durationSec":351.408,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":3.68,"text":"Canberra is the capital of Australia, and it is a planned city."},{"start":4.38,"end":7.76,"text":"People drew the city on paper first, and then they built it."},{"start":8.28,"end":8.48,"text":"Why?"},{"start":9.34,"end":14.08,"text":"When Australia became one country, Sydney and Melbourne both wanted to be the capital."},{"start":14.72,"end":18.08,"text":"They could not agree, so the government built a new city between them."},{"start":18.86,"end":22.94,"text":"In 1912, two Americans won a competition to draw the new city."},{"start":23.56,"end":27.78,"text":"They were a husband and wife, Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin."},{"start":28.34,"end":30.94,"text":"For the Griffins, a lake was the heart of the city."},{"start":31.62,"end":33.9,"text":"The government buildings are on one side of the lake."},{"start":34.32,"end":36.14,"text":"The city centre is on the other side."},{"start":36.72,"end":38.52,"text":"They look at each other across the water."},{"start":39.54,"end":41.34,"text":"But Canberra had only a small river."},{"start":41.9,"end":45.98,"text":"So workers built a dam, a big wall that stops a river."},{"start":46.76,"end":49.22,"text":"In 1963, they closed its gates."},{"start":49.86,"end":53.16,"text":"The river water stopped at the wall, and slowly it made a lake."},{"start":53.56,"end":56.08,"text":"By April 1964, the lake was full."},{"start":56.51,"end":61.18,"text":"It is 11 kilometres long, and its name is Lake Burley Griffin."},{"start":62.04,"end":64.44,"text":"Today the lake is the weekend park for the city."},{"start":64.92,"end":68.22,"text":"The most popular walk and bike ride is the bridge-to-bridge loop."},{"start":68.88,"end":71.94,"text":"It is a 5-kilometre circle that goes over two bridges."},{"start":72.88,"end":76.64,"text":"People from rowing clubs train in long, thin boats at the west end."},{"start":77.34,"end":81.06,"text":"By the water there are free electric barbecues and picnic tables."},{"start":82.04,"end":84.64,"text":"So families bring food and cook their lunch there."},{"start":85.5,"end":89.36,"text":"Australians love coffee, and this love started with migrants."},{"start":90.38,"end":92.66,"text":"Migrants are people who move to live in a new country."},{"start":93.56,"end":97.72,"text":"After 1945, many people came to Australia from Italy and Greece."},{"start":98.48,"end":100.28,"text":"They brought espresso machines with them."},{"start":100.92,"end":103.9,"text":"These machines push hot water through coffee very fast."},{"start":104.6,"end":107.96,"text":"They make a small, strong coffee called an espresso."},{"start":109.08,"end":114.16,"text":"Greek families put the machines in their milk bars, small shops for drinks and snacks."},{"start":114.16,"end":118.14,"text":"Italian families opened espresso cafés in Melbourne."},{"start":118.14,"end":121.86,"text":"The most famous Australian coffee is the flat white."},{"start":122.56,"end":126.1,"text":"It is espresso with hot milk and a little foam on top."},{"start":127.12,"end":129.64,"text":"Foam is milk with a lot of air in it."},{"start":130.1,"end":132.94,"text":"There is very little foam, so the top looks flat."},{"start":133.6,"end":137.78,"text":"Australia and New Zealand both say they made the first one, in the 1980s."},{"start":138.42,"end":143.1,"text":"Today a flat white costs about $6 in a big city café."},{"start":143.1,"end":146.8,"text":"Most cafés in Australia are small local shops, not big chains."},{"start":147.48,"end":150.2,"text":"A chain is a company with the same shop in many places."},{"start":150.2,"end":155.1,"text":"Australians already had good local cafés, so big chains found it hard."},{"start":156.06,"end":160.26,"text":"In 2008, Starbucks closed more than 60 of its shops in Australia."},{"start":161.1,"end":163.98,"text":"People said its drinks were too sweet and too expensive."},{"start":164.94,"end":168.86,"text":"A sausage sizzle is a barbecue that sells food to raise money for a group."},{"start":169.74,"end":172.24,"text":"Sizzle is the sound of food cooking on a hot plate."},{"start":173.24,"end":175.54,"text":"You get one sausage in a slice of white bread."},{"start":176.38,"end":178.8,"text":"Many people add fried onions and tomato sauce."},{"start":179.56,"end":183.06,"text":"The most famous ones are outside Bunnings, a big hardware shop."},{"start":183.78,"end":187.16,"text":"A hardware shop sells tools and things for your house and garden."},{"start":187.64,"end":191.58,"text":"On weekends, a local group, like a school or a sports club, cooks there."},{"start":192.26,"end":195.86,"text":"Bunnings takes no money from the group, so the group keeps it all."},{"start":196.52,"end":200.54,"text":"A sausage costs $3.50 and a drink costs $2."},{"start":201.34,"end":205.46,"text":"Sausage sizzles also appear on election day, the day people vote."},{"start":206.1,"end":209.24,"text":"They are at polling places, the places where people vote."},{"start":209.98,"end":210.46,"text":"Why there?"},{"start":211,"end":213.1,"text":"In Australia, voting is compulsory."},{"start":213.74,"end":217.46,"text":"Every Australian citizen aged 18 or older must vote."},{"start":218.16,"end":220.34,"text":"People who do not vote can get a fine."},{"start":220.8,"end":223.48,"text":"A fine is money you pay when you break a rule."},{"start":224.36,"end":227.97,"text":"Because of this law, more than 90% of people vote in national elections."},{"start":227.96,"end":232.68,"text":"Elections are on Saturdays, and people often vote in schools."},{"start":233.32,"end":237.3,"text":"Almost everyone comes, so school parents cook for the long lines."},{"start":237.88,"end":239.14,"text":"The money helps the school."},{"start":240.18,"end":242.42,"text":"Australians call this a democracy sausage."},{"start":243.04,"end":246.22,"text":"In a democracy, people choose their leaders by voting."},{"start":246.96,"end":250.88,"text":"In 2016, Australian word experts made it their Word of the Year."},{"start":251.6,"end":254,"text":"Many people get work emails and messages at night."},{"start":254.72,"end":257.34,"text":"In Australia, a law now helps with this."},{"start":257.74,"end":259.4,"text":"It is called the right to disconnect."},{"start":260.46,"end":264.56,"text":"When you disconnect from work, you stop all work, even on your phone."},{"start":265.44,"end":271.62,"text":"The law started on 26 August 2024 for companies with 15 or more workers."},{"start":272.2,"end":276.32,"text":"From 26 August 2025, it also covers small businesses."},{"start":277.1,"end":279.54,"text":"So now almost every worker has this right."},{"start":280.44,"end":284.08,"text":"After work, you can choose not to read or answer work messages."},{"start":284.88,"end":289.14,"text":"Your boss can still send them, but you can wait until your next work day."},{"start":290.06,"end":293.48,"text":"There is one rule: your choice must not be unreasonable."},{"start":294.32,"end":296.82,"text":"Unreasonable is another way to say not fair."},{"start":297.56,"end":302.3,"text":"For example, at 9:30 pm your manager emails to move tomorrow's meeting."},{"start":303,"end":305.94,"text":"It is not urgent: it does not need an answer now."},{"start":306.26,"end":307.74,"text":"So you can read it in the morning."},{"start":308.38,"end":311.62,"text":"But sometimes waiting is not fair, and you should answer."},{"start":312.46,"end":314.5,"text":"For example, someone could get hurt."},{"start":315.2,"end":317.34,"text":"Or your job pays you extra to be on call."},{"start":318.4,"end":321.16,"text":"When you are on call, you are ready to work at any time."},{"start":322.28,"end":323.64,"text":"Why do Australians care so much?"},{"start":324.36,"end":326.76,"text":"Many people work extra hours for no money."},{"start":327.46,"end":329.56,"text":"This is called unpaid overtime."},{"start":329.86,"end":336.36,"text":"In 2025, a study found that workers did 3.6 hours of unpaid overtime every week."},{"start":336.88,"end":341.88,"text":"That is 173 hours a year, or more than four weeks of free work."},{"start":342.68,"end":344.82,"text":"Australians love their evenings and weekends."},{"start":345.46,"end":347.86,"text":"They use them for sport, the beach and family."},{"start":348.56,"end":350.96,"text":"This law helps them keep those hours free."}],"words":[{"text":"Canberra","start":0,"end":0.4},{"text":"is","start":0.4,"end":0.82},{"text":"the","start":0.82,"end":0.96},{"text":"capital","start":0.96,"end":1.28},{"text":"of","start":1.28,"end":1.48},{"text":"Australia","start":1.48,"end":1.86},{"text":"and","start":2.46,"end":2.52},{"text":"it","start":2.52,"end":2.62},{"text":"is","start":2.62,"end":2.74},{"text":"a","start":2.74,"end":2.92},{"text":"planned","start":2.92,"end":3.24},{"text":"city","start":3.24,"end":3.68},{"text":"People","start":4.38,"end":4.56},{"text":"drew","start":4.56,"end":4.82},{"text":"the","start":4.82,"end":5.02},{"text":"city","start":5.02,"end":5.26},{"text":"on","start":5.26,"end":5.48},{"text":"paper","start":5.48,"end":5.76},{"text":"first","start":5.76,"end":6.18},{"text":"and","start":6.62,"end":6.8},{"text":"then","start":6.8,"end":6.98},{"text":"they","start":6.98,"end":7.18},{"text":"built","start":7.18,"end":7.36},{"text":"it","start":7.36,"end":7.76},{"text":"Why","start":8.28,"end":8.48},{"text":"When","start":9.34,"end":9.44},{"text":"Australia","start":9.44,"end":9.94},{"text":"became","start":9.94,"end":10.32},{"text":"one","start":10.32,"end":10.62},{"text":"country","start":10.62,"end":11.12},{"text":"Sydney","start":11.8,"end":11.92},{"text":"and","start":11.92,"end":12.2},{"text":"Melbourne","start":12.2,"end":12.56},{"text":"both","start":12.56,"end":13.02},{"text":"wanted","start":13.02,"end":13.32}